SUBROUTINE
.in +5
.br
.bd
getpass
- read a password without echoing it
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd
#include <crypt.h>
.sp
.bd 2
char *getpass(prompt)
.br
char *prompt;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTIONS
.in +5
.br
Getpass writes
.bd
prompt
on the standard error, turns off the terminal's echo, reads a line
from the standard input, turns the echo back on and returns a
pointer to the line read, without its newline.  The prompt is
written wherever the output is sent; the password is not echoed.
.sp
A carriage return is written after the line, because the one the
user typed was not echoed and the cursor would otherwise be left
sitting after the prompt.
.sp
The line is read from file descriptor 0 and the terminal settings
are changed on file descriptor 0.
.sp
If the terminal cannot be asked for its settings, or will not take
them back, the echo is left as it was and the password is read with
the echo on.  Nothing is written to the standard error about it and
there is no error return; a caller that must not echo has to check
the terminal itself.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The answer is in a static buffer of 128 characters that the next
call overwrites.  A longer line is truncated, not overrun.
.sp
v7's getpass opened /dev/tty and read the password from there, so
that a password could be typed even with the standard input
redirected.  There is no /dev/tty on this machine and this reads
descriptor 0, as
.bd
login (1)
does.
.sp
Getpass is in libc - lib/libc/getpass.c.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
